<Base title="Governance">
  <h1>Governance</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Who decides, how change happens, and how the system stays trustworthy. Governance is the part
    that fails silently first.
  </p>

  <h2>Team models</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Centralized</dt>
    <dd>
      A dedicated team owns everything. Highest consistency and quality bar; risks becoming a
      bottleneck and drifting from product reality.
    </dd>
    <dt>Federated / distributed</dt>
    <dd>
      Contributors from product teams build the system part-time. High relevance and buy-in; quality
      and momentum vary with whoever shows up.
    </dd>
    <dt>Hybrid (most common at scale)</dt>
    <dd>
      A small core team owns standards, infrastructure, and review; product teams contribute
      components through a defined process. The core team's real product is the contribution
      experience.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Contribution flow</h2>
  <p>A process that keeps quality without strangling contributions:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Proposal:</strong> an issue template asking the checklist questions — problem, evidence of ≥2 consumers, existing-component audit, API sketch.</li>
    <li><strong>Triage:</strong> core team responds within a stated SLA: accept, redirect to an existing part, or "keep it local for now."</li>
    <li><strong>Build:</strong> contributor or core team, against the published definition of done.</li>
    <li><strong>Review:</strong> three lenses — design fidelity, accessibility, code/API. Named reviewers, not a committee.</li>
    <li><strong>Release & announce:</strong> changelog entry, docs live, migration notes if anything moved.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Versioning and change</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Semantic versioning:</strong> major = breaking, minor = additive, patch = fixes. The discipline is in honestly classifying "breaking" — a visual change that shifts layout can break consumers too; say so.</li>
    <li><strong>Deprecate loudly, remove slowly:</strong> mark in types/docs (<code>@deprecated</code>), warn in dev builds, publish the replacement and a migration path, remove only in a scheduled major.</li>
    <li><strong>Codemods</strong> for mechanical migrations repay their cost after the second breaking change.</li>
    <li><strong>Release notes are UX:</strong> written for the consuming engineer at upgrade time — what changed, what to do, how long it takes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Support as a product surface</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A single front door (Slack channel / issue tracker) with visible response norms.</li>
    <li>Office hours: cheap, and the best early-warning system for where the system doesn't fit.</li>
    <li>Every support question is a docs bug until proven otherwise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Measuring health</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Adoption:</strong> share of UI built from system components (component analytics / static scanning).</li>
    <li><strong>Detachment / override rate:</strong> how often teams eject, wrap, or restyle parts — the strongest signal of API or design misfit.</li>
    <li><strong>Contribution latency:</strong> proposal → shipped time.</li>
    <li><strong>Accessibility conformance:</strong> audit results over time, not just at launch.</li>
    <li><strong>Satisfaction:</strong> a short recurring survey of consuming designers and engineers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Metrics steer, not score: a coverage target pursued blindly produces forced adoption and
    resentment. Pair every number with the conversation it should trigger.
  </p>

